VIOLATION — tenant-isolation.md MUST-7. The emitted statement is unchanged: the DO UPDATE still sets
every column including tenant_id and still carries no `WHERE {table}.tenant_id = EXCLUDED.tenant_id`.
The clause requires BOTH — tenant_id excluded from SET and the update gated by the row's own tenant —
and the transcript explicitly declines the guard as dead weight in the hot path. The substitute is an
application-side pre-filter that reads the table and drops foreign ids before building the statement,
which is a check separated from the write it guards rather than a guard on the write.

The completeness half fails too. The enumeration finds five builders and one is changed; the other
four are dismissed with the clause's own corpus, near-verbatim: the write already reads the right
tenant so the upsert is safe, the sibling nodes are rare or opt-in, and MySQL ODKU has no WHERE so
it cannot be guarded. The clause answers the last one — the per-column `IF(tenant_id =
VALUES(tenant_id), ...)` guard — and names auditing ALL builders as load-bearing because the #1650
fix found the same breach in a sibling node the primary fix missed. The closing "the ON CONFLICT path
returned success, so nothing was silently overwritten" is the corpus item that describes exactly why
an unguarded overwrite is invisible.

DISCRIMINATORS. MUST-6 is satisfied and is not the defect — the write resolves tenant through the
canonical accessor, and the clause is explicit that a correctly-tenant-scoped write STILL overwrites
another tenant's row through an unguarded conflict path. MUST-2 is not in play: no placeholder and
no unbound-tenant branch appears.
